      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 1 -->
    <section class="sec" id="why-test">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>1. The Clinical Importance of Purposeful Fertility Diagnostics</h2>
        <p>Achieving conception requires a synchronized sequence of biological events: mature egg release, adequate sperm count and motility, patent fallopian tubes, and a receptive uterine lining. When a couple experiences difficulty conceiving after 12 months of regular, unprotected intercourse (or 6 months if the female partner is aged 35 or older), systematic diagnostic testing is essential.</p>
        <p>At Kamran Fertility Clinic, Dr. Kamran Saleem approaches reproductive diagnostics with strict medical integrity. Rather than subjecting couples to exhaustive, repetitive, or non-evidence-based testing panels, our diagnostic strategy is guided by three core principles:</p>
        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="card"><h3>Targeted Investigation</h3><p>Diagnostic tests are selected based on medical history, physical evaluation, and clinical indications—ensuring every investigation yields actionable diagnostic value.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>Dual-Partner Concurrent Testing</h3><p>Male and female partners are evaluated simultaneously from the first consultation, recognizing that male factor subfertility contributes to approximately 40% to 50% of all reproductive challenges.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>Diagnostic Precision First</h3><p>Establishing an accurate diagnosis before initiating treatment prevents costly, unnecessary interventions and allows for personalized protocol selection (e.g., distinguishing candidates for Ovulation Induction/IUI from those requiring ICSI or Micro-TESE).</p></div>
        </div>

        <!-- ADDED -->
        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Who should not wait for the 12-month mark</h3>
          <p>Book an earlier consultation if the woman is 40 or older, periods are very irregular or absent, or there is a history of PCOS, endometriosis, pelvic infection or surgery, repeated miscarriage, cancer treatment, or a previous abnormal semen analysis. If you are planning fertility preservation, see <a href={L.freezing}>Egg &amp; Sperm Freezing</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 MALE -->
    <section class="sec sec-tint" id="male-tests">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>2. Male Fertility Diagnostic Suite: WHO 6th Edition Standards &amp; Advanced Sperm Testing</h2>
        <p>Male reproductive health requires accurate, standardized laboratory evaluation. As a specialist holding a UK Diploma in Genito-Urinary Medicine (GUM) &amp; Venereology from the University of Liverpool, Dr. Kamran Saleem provides specialized expertise in evaluating sperm function, reproductive tract infections, and male endocrine disorders. Start with the <a href={L.maleAssess}>Male Fertility Assessment</a>.</p>

        <ol class="flow">
          <li><strong>Step 1: WHO 6th Ed Semen Analysis</strong><span>Volume, Concentration, Motility, Strict Kruger Morphology</span></li>
          <li><strong>Step 2: Advanced Sperm DFI</strong><span>SCSA/TUNEL Assay for Chromatin Integrity &amp; Oxidative Damage</span></li>
          <li><strong>Step 3: Male Hormonal &amp; Scrotal Doppler</strong><span>Serum Testosterone, FSH, LH &amp; Varicocele Ultrasound Evaluation</span></li>
        </ol>

        <h3 id="semen-analysis">A. Semen Analysis (WHO 6th Edition Laboratory Manual Standards)</h3>
        <p>Semen analysis remains the cornerstone of male fertility evaluation. Our diagnostic laboratory adheres strictly to the WHO 6th Edition (2021) guidelines, utilizing standardized counting chambers and Kruger strict morphology criteria. <a href={L.semen}>Read the full semen analysis guide</a>.</p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Semen Parameter</th><th>WHO 6th Edition Reference Limit</th><th>Clinical Significance &amp; Diagnostic Interpretation</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {who.map(([p, r, s]) => <tr><th scope="row">{p}</th><td class="nowrap">{r}</td><td>{s}</td></tr>)}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 id="sperm-dfi">B. Sperm DNA Fragmentation Index (DFI) Testing</h3>
        <p>Standard semen analysis evaluates sperm count and movement but does not assess genetic material integrity. Sperm DNA Fragmentation Index (DFI) measures single- and double-strand DNA breaks within the sperm head using advanced assays (SCSA, TUNEL, or Sperm Chromatin Dispersion).</p>
        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="card tier ok"><h4>Normal DFI (&lt; 15%)</h4><p>High genetic integrity; excellent prognosis for natural conception, IUI, and conventional IVF.</p></div>
          <div class="card tier mid"><h4>Moderate DFI (15% to 30%)</h4><p>Mild to moderate chromatin damage; lifestyle modification, antioxidant therapy, and varicocele treatment advised.</p></div>
          <div class="card tier high"><h4>High DFI (&gt; 30%)</h4><p>Significant DNA damage linked to recurrent unexplained IVF/ICSI fertilization failure, poor blastocyst development, and early pregnancy loss. Indicates the need for specialized sperm selection techniques (PICSI/IMSI) or testicular sperm extraction (TESA). See <a href={L.icsi}>ICSI</a>.</p></div>
        </div>

        <h3 id="semen-culture">C. Semen Culture &amp; Sensitivity Testing</h3>
        <p>Given Dr. Kamran Saleem’s specialized background in Genito-Urinary Medicine, semen culture testing is performed whenever leukocytospermia, altered semen pH, or symptoms of urogenital infection are detected. Identifying and treating subclinical bacterial infections (such as Ureaplasma, Mycoplasma, or E. coli) with targeted antibiotics restores sperm motility and prevents infection transmission.</p>

        <h3 id="male-hormones">D. Male Reproductive Hormonal Panel &amp; Scrotal Doppler Ultrasound</h3>
        <p>When severe oligozoospermia or azoospermia is diagnosed, secondary blood testing and imaging are performed:</p>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li><strong>Serum Testosterone, FSH, LH, and Prolactin:</strong> Differentiates Obstructive Azoospermia (OA) (normal hormones, physical blockage) from Non-Obstructive Azoospermia (NOA) (elevated FSH/LH indicating primary testicular failure, or low FSH/LH indicating hypogonadotropic hypogonadism).</li>
          <li><strong>Scrotal Color Doppler Ultrasound:</strong> Evaluates testicular volume, detects epididymal cysts or vas deferens absence, and identifies subclinical varicoceles (dilated scrotal veins that elevate testicular temperature and impair spermatogenesis).</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- ADDED -->
        <h3 id="male-genetic">E. Genetic Tests for Severe Male Factor</h3>
        <p>When a man has azoospermia or a very low sperm count, a genetic blood test may be advised before treatment. A karyotype checks the chromosomes, and a Y-chromosome microdeletion test looks for missing segments that control sperm production. If the vas deferens is absent (CBAVD), CFTR gene testing is considered. These results affect what is possible with <a href={L.maleTx}>surgical sperm retrieval</a> and whether <a href={L.pgt}>embryo genetic testing (PGT)</a> is worth discussing.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 FEMALE -->
    <section class="sec" id="female-tests">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>3. Female Fertility Diagnostic Suite: Ovarian Reserve, Hormones &amp; Pelvic Imaging</h2>
        <p>Female reproductive diagnostics evaluate ovarian egg quantity and quality, ovulatory regularity, fallopian tube patency, and uterine cavity receptivity. Start with the <a href={L.femaleAssess}>Female Fertility Assessment</a>.</p>

        <ol class="flow">
          <li><strong>Phase 1: Ovarian Reserve &amp; Basal Hormones</strong><span>AMH Blood Test + Day 2/3 FSH, LH &amp; E2</span></li>
          <li><strong>Phase 2: Tubal Patency Screening</strong><span>HSG X-Ray or 3D/4D HyCoSy Scan</span></li>
          <li><strong>Phase 3: Pelvic Ultrasound &amp; Ovulation</strong><span>Transvaginal Ultrasound (AFC &amp; Cavity)</span></li>
        </ol>

        <h3 id="ovarian-reserve">A. Ovarian Reserve Evaluation (AMH, Basal Hormones &amp; AFC)</h3>
        <p>Assessing ovarian reserve provides vital prognostic insight into a woman's remaining egg supply and expected response to fertility medications. <a href={L.hormonal}>Read the full hormonal analysis guide</a>.</p>
        <p><strong>Serum Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH):</strong> AMH is produced by small antral follicles in the ovaries and can be tested on any day of the menstrual cycle (unaffected by hormonal contraceptives or cycle phase).</p>
        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="card tier ok"><h4>Optimal Reserve (1.5 to 4.0 ng/mL)</h4><p>Normal egg supply; predicts good response to Ovulation Induction and IVF stimulation.</p></div>
          <div class="card tier mid"><h4>High Reserve (&gt; 4.0 to 7.0+ ng/mL)</h4><p>Characteristic of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS); requires low-dose stimulation protocols to prevent Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome (OHSS). See <a href={L.pcos}>Ovulation Induction &amp; PCOS</a>.</p></div>
          <div class="card tier high"><h4>Diminished Ovarian Reserve (DOR) (&lt; 1.0 ng/mL)</h4><p>Reduced egg quantity; indicates the need for timely intervention and individualized stimulation protocols.</p></div>
        </div>
        <h4 class="mt">Cycle Day 2 or 3 Basal Hormonal Panel</h4>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li><strong>Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH) &amp; Luteinizing Hormone (LH):</strong> Basal FSH &gt; 10–12 IU/L suggests declining ovarian responsiveness; an LH-to-FSH ratio &gt; 2:1 is indicative of PCOS.</li>
          <li><strong>Serum Estradiol (E2):</strong> Baseline estrogen levels &lt; 50 pg/mL confirm ovarian quiescence prior to starting stimulation.</li>
          <li><strong>Antral Follicle Count (AFC) via Transvaginal Ultrasound:</strong> Performed on Cycle Day 2, 3, or 4 to physically count visible 2mm–10mm resting follicles in both ovaries. An AFC of 10 to 20 total antral follicles reflects healthy reserve.</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- ADDED -->
        <h3 id="ovulation-tracking">B. Ovulation Tracking</h3>
        <p>Ovulation tracking confirms whether, and when, you release an egg. Depending on your cycle, it can combine cycle charting, home LH kits, and the mid-luteal progesterone blood test described below. It is also used to time <a href={L.iui}>IUI</a> and to monitor response during <a href={L.pcos}>ovulation induction</a>. <a href={L.ovulation}>Read the full ovulation tracking guide</a>.</p>

        <h3 id="tubal-patency">C. Fallopian Tube Patency &amp; Pelvic Screening (HSG &amp; HyCoSy)</h3>
        <p>Unobstructed, healthy fallopian tubes are required for natural conception and IUI, as fertilization takes place within the ampullary region of the tube.</p>
        <div class="grid2">
          <div class="card"><h4>Hysterosalpingogram (HSG)</h4><p>A specialized diagnostic fluoroscopic X-ray performed between Cycle Days 6 and 10. Radio-opaque contrast dye is gently instilled through the cervix into the uterine cavity, taking real-time images as the dye fills the uterus, flows through the fallopian tubes, and spills into the peritoneal cavity. HSG confirms tubal patency and identifies uterine cavity abnormalities (such as endometrial polyps, submucosal fibroids, or uterine septa).</p></div>
          <div class="card"><h4>Hysterosalpingo-Contrast Sonography (HyCoSy)</h4><p>An advanced, comfortable 3D/4D transvaginal ultrasound alternative that uses a specialized microbubble foam contrast agent to visualize tubal flow without X-ray radiation exposure.</p></div>
        </div>

        <h3 id="endocrine">D. Endocrine, Ovulatory &amp; Metabolic Screening</h3>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li><strong>Day 21 Serum Progesterone (Mid-Luteal Phase):</strong> Tested 7 days prior to anticipated menstruation (Day 21 of a 28-day cycle). A value &gt; 10 ng/mL confirms successful ovulation and adequate luteal phase corpus luteum function.</li>
          <li><strong>Serum Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH) &amp; Prolactin:</strong> Subclinical hypothyroidism (TSH &gt; 2.5 mIU/L) or hyperprolactinemia (elevated prolactin) directly interferes with pulsatile GnRH secretion, causing anovulation or luteal phase defect.</li>
          <li><strong>Fasting Insulin &amp; Glucose Ratio:</strong> Evaluates insulin resistance in women with PCOS, guiding targeted metabolic treatment with Metformin or Myo-Inositol.</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- ADDED -->
        <h3 id="pre-treatment">E. Pre-Treatment Screening</h3>
        <p>Before IUI, IVF or ICSI, the clinic may also check blood group, blood count, rubella immunity, and screening for HIV, hepatitis B, hepatitis C and syphilis in both partners, and other tests such as vitamin D only where clinically indicated. This protects you, your partner and any future pregnancy, and is part of safe treatment rather than a search for the cause of infertility.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 PREP -->
    <section class="sec sec-tint" id="preparation">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>4. Patient Preparation Guidelines &amp; Test Execution Protocol</h2>
        <p>To ensure absolute accuracy and prevent test invalidation, patients should follow standardized preparation instructions:</p>
        <div class="grid2">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Male Preparation Guidelines (Semen Analysis &amp; DFI)</h3>
            <ul class="ticks">
              <li><strong>Abstinence Window:</strong> Strictly 2 to 5 days of ejaculatory abstinence prior to sample collection. Abstinence periods &lt; 2 days may artificially lower sperm volume and count; abstinence &gt; 5 days leads to sperm accumulation, reduced motility, and increased DNA fragmentation.</li>
              <li><strong>Collection Protocols:</strong> Samples are collected on-site in dedicated, private collection suites across our DHA Defence, Gulberg, or Shalamar clinics into sterile, non-toxic containers.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Female Preparation Guidelines (Bloods, Imaging &amp; HSG)</h3>
            <ul class="ticks">
              <li><strong>Basal Hormone Testing:</strong> Serum FSH, LH, and Estradiol MUST be drawn strictly on Cycle Day 2 or 3 of natural menstrual bleeding.</li>
              <li><strong>AMH Testing:</strong> Can be drawn on any calendar day regardless of menstrual cycle phase.</li>
              <li><strong>HSG / HyCoSy Procedure Window:</strong> Scheduled strictly between Cycle Days 6 and 10 (after menstrual bleeding has completely stopped, but prior to ovulation) to ensure the uterine lining is thin and prevent accidental disruption of an early unconfirmed pregnancy.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

